[Music]  hi everyone and welcome to lesson three  this lesson will be similar to the  previous lesson but going over how to  plot and customize  box plots another very common plot type  used in ecology when you want to  visualize the association  of a continuous variable between various  categorical groups  these are also called box and whisker  plots first we'll load the same data as  before  the plant growth data and just as a  quick reminder  the one column the weight column  represents the dried biomass weight  in grams of each plant and group  represents three different treatments  that were used when growing the plant  and these data include 30 different  samples  okay so let's go on and make our first  box plot and let's do that by comparing  the weight values between  each of these treatments and so what's  neat is that the regular plot function  will automatically create a box plot if  the x-axis  variable is categorical and so if we  look at the group variable in our data  set  plant growth  we can see that this is a categorical  factor variable  with the levels control treatment 1 and  treatment 2.  so if we do plot  weight  as a function of group  theta equals plant growth  and run that r will automatically create  a nice box plot showing weight as the  y-axis and these different groups  as the x-axis and there it is so this  isn't a statistics class  but here's a quick overview of the  different parts of the block box plot  for your reference so the dark line  within each of these groups  represents the median value the gray box  is the interquartile range of your data  of each of these categories so that  means between the 25th  and the 75th quartile so 50  of all of your data points lie within  this gray area then the whiskers or  these lines  mark the maximum and minimum range of  the data  excluding outliers the outliers are  calculated so like this point right here  is considered  technically considered an outlier is  calculated as  any points that are greater or less than  the interquartile range  times 1.5 so essentially it provides a  standardized method for labeling points  if they're very far from  all the other points but it's a bit more  of a conceptual  theoretical uh question as to whether  these are  actually treated as outliers or not but  this isn't a statistics course so i'm  not really going to go into that  but for now i'm just explaining what  these different aspects of the box plot  represent  so now as with the scatter plots we can  play around with these axes a bit  so let's copy and paste that here  and let's add in let's modify the x  and the y labels  so x lab let's do treatment  group y  lab equals dried biomass  weight in grams so if we run that there  we go  okay but the the labels for each  individual treatment group  are still not very clear and to change  those let's actually change the data  themselves to have category names that  are a bit clearer  and so we can look at that by using the  levels function  and plant growth  within groups so again this is our  vector of groups and it's a factor  variable because you can see that here  we have these three different levels  and the levels function just kind of  extracts those three levels so we can  look at them  control or ctrl trt1  and trt2 so we can replace those  with some names that are a little bit  more meaningful so let's say that we  know treatment 1 is low sunlight  and treatment 2 is high sunlight  or a lot of sunlight so we could maybe  rename these as  levels so actually we don't have to  rewrite this  so into so this function extracts the  levels  but we can modify those levels by  defining new levels right into this  function  like that so let's define the new level  names as  control  highlight  and low light  okay something like that maybe we'll add  a little dash here  great so now i ran that row and now  let's just take a look  at there you go  so see these different levels have been  renamed accordingly  so now if we run this same set of code  one more time  run it right here now you can see that  these labels have been  renamed accordingly so next let's  customize the design of the box plot a  bit more by changing the color of the  boxes as i mentioned in the previous  lesson you should always strive for  black and white or grayscale unless  color is  absolutely necessary for visualizing  your data and and that definitely  applies to this case  but i'm just going to show it to you  here how to modify the colors just for  completeness so let's just copy that  again  here and we will add in  simple argument of call  equals and again we can add in a custom  color  such as blue you can add hex code colors  this will take several different forms  of color values but we can also use  integer values that represent a series  of different built-in colors so in this  case let's do  call equals four and when we run that  we get this nice blue color that fills  in all the boxes  you can also remove the border around  the boxes using  box lty  and that just represents box line type  equals zero  so that removes this black border around  the boxes we can make the  whiskers instead of having them be  dotted we can make them solid  using the  whisk lty  equals one like that and then we can  also make all the lines just a bit  thicker  so we can do that using whisk lwd  line width equals let's say 1.5  and then the end this top is also called  the staple  so we can say staple line width  equals 1.5  so that just made the lines just a  little bit thicker kind of making it all  a little bit more visually appealing  and i kind of tend to prefer this type  of look for a bit  of a cleaner box plot all right so now  let's  change the orientation of the box plot  again let's  copy that over and what i mean by  orientation is just  add in this argument horizontal  equals true and that just turns  everything sideways in some cases box  plots for certain types of data or when  you have a lot of different categories  it becomes a little bit neater to have  the box plots go horizontally like that  so great that's it for this lesson so  far these lessons should  have given you everything you need for  creating the two most common types of  plots in ecology  using r's built-in plot function in  summary in this lesson we went over  building a box plot the using just the  simple plot  function and the only difference is that  the  data on the x-axis are now categorical  instead  of continuous we went over the different  parts of the box plot and what they  represent  and how to change the axis labels and  axis names on the box plot how to  customize the color and visual style of  the boxes and the whiskers  and finally how to turn the boxes on  their side and with that  i hope you enjoyed this lesson in the  next lesson i'm going to go over  a simple method for prototyping and  saving the plots that you make in  r

